#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ragnarok-agent: spawn a Claude Code, Grok, Muse or Codex agent with the owner's harness and track it end to end.

Claude is plain `claude -p` in a folder, so ~/.claude/settings.json applies as in any session: receipts on every
tool call, memory arm, drift check, evidence audit (which can block and send it back), auto permission mode with its
safety check. Grok is plain `grok -p` in that folder. Grok loads those same Claude hooks (its default), approves its
own tool calls, and does not stop to ask for a plan. Muse is plain `muse exec --json` in that folder (Muse Code,
muse-wonder): it runs unattended with --yolo (no approval prompts, workspace trusted for the run) and request_user_input
withheld, and this program translates its MSP JSONL stream into the same tracker.
This program only starts the process
detached (it survives Ragnarok restarting), records every event, and keeps a live state file the Agents tab reads.

  ragnarok-agent start --who claude|grok|muse|codex --cwd DIR [--provider P] [--model M] [--name N] [--plain] [--checklist FILE] [--room-task ID] [--handoff p1,p2|--no-handoff] [--] "task"
  ragnarok-agent resume ID [--who claude|grok|muse|codex] [--provider P] [--model M] [--approve-human|--no-approve-human] ["answer / context"]
  ragnarok-agent handoff ID [--who claude|grok|muse|codex] [--provider P] [--model M] [--handoff p1,p2|--no-handoff] ["note"]
  ragnarok-agent list                                            recent runs, newest first
  ragnarok-agent show ID                                         one run's state (JSON)
  ragnarok-agent stop [--force] ID                               stop it; it stays stopped (SIGTERM, or SIGKILL with --force, including a provider the first stop left alive)
  ragnarok-agent run ID                                          (internal) the worker
  ragnarok-agent selfcheck                                       fake CLIs only; no model call
  --help and -h print this usage and exit. A task that begins with a dash must come after --. Stdin and a leading space count.

PROVED MODE (every runner, the default; --plain turns it off). The conveyor's rule on top of the harness: the agent's
own "done" is not believed. It writes a checklist where every line carries a shell command that proves it:
  - [ ] <what must be true> :: verify: <command>  [:: expect: <regex>]      - [ ] <claim> :: verify: HUMAN
The checklist is frozen the first time it is written (later edits can add lines, never drop or change one). When the
agent says it's done, this program runs every command itself, read-only (macOS sandbox: no file writes outside temp
folders, no ssh/scp/rsync). A failed check goes back to the same agent with the output. A round whose failing
checks print the same output as the last round, or 4 rounds, is a dead end, and that stuck checklist is not handed
to the next provider. A check the sandbox cannot run (ssh, scp, sftp, rsync), or a whole-repo git status or diff,
is left for the owner and is not scored as a failure. When every command passes, a fresh critic (read-only tools) tries
to show the work is wrong; its refutations go back too. Outcomes: proved, needs you (HUMAN or an unrunnable check),
dead end.

Files, per run: ~/.ragnarok/agents/<id>/
  task.txt        what was asked          events.jsonl   every stream event, raw
  state.json      live tracker state      worker.log     the worker's own errors
  checklist.md    the agent's proofs      checklist.frozen  the copy taken when it was first written
  model-stderr.log  the model's own stderr, full, including a process that exited 0
"""
import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
